{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Evidence-Centered Design for Prototypes","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting evidence-centered design for prototypes to prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-01","statement":"State the three ECD layers used here: claim (what the learner knows or can do), evidence (the observable that would reveal it), task (the screen that elicits the observable)"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-02","statement":"Audit a traceability matrix row for the two classic failures: an unobservable evidence cell and a task that cannot elicit the named observable"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-03","statement":"Rewrite an internal-state evidence cell (\"feels confident\") as a scorable behavior"},{"id":"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-04","statement":"Reject task features that produce data unrelated to the claim"}],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"02","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/07/01"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_calibration"],"lo_practice_map":{"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-01":["ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_calibration"],"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-02":["ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_annotation_signal"],"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-03":["ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_artifact_signal"],"ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-04":["ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_ai_boundary"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Evidence-Centered Design for Prototypes\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Evidence Centered Design For Prototypes, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-02-evidence-centered-design-for-prototypes.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Evidence-Centered Design for Prototypes: Evidence Centered Design For Prototypes, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA prototype screen can look finished while quietly failing the one job that justifies it: producing evidence that a learner can do what you claim. Evidence-Centered Design (ECD), articulated by Mislevy and colleagues, gives you a discipline for closing the gap between what you assert and what your screens can actually show. Instead of building tasks and then guessing what they measure, ECD makes you reason backward from the claim to the evidence to the task. For a learning designer, the README traceability matrix is where that backward chain becomes inspectable: every screen must trace to an observable, and every observable must trace to a knowledge claim.\n\nThis section matters because misalignment is invisible until someone audits it. A drag-the-label task feels like it measures interpretation; on inspection it may only measure whether the learner can read labels. ECD is the tool that surfaces that mismatch before a reviewer, or a real learner, does.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- State the three ECD layers used here: claim (what the learner knows or can do), evidence (the observable that would reveal it), task (the screen that elicits the observable).\n- Audit a traceability matrix row for the two classic failures: an unobservable evidence cell and a task that cannot elicit the named observable.\n- Rewrite an internal-state evidence cell (\"feels confident\") as a scorable behavior.\n- Reject task features that produce data unrelated to the claim.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teammate shows you a slick prototype: a drag-the-label screen where learners drag terms onto a residual plot. The matrix row reads: claim, \"learner can interpret a residual plot\"; evidence, \"learner feels confident about the plot\"; task, \"drag-the-label screen.\" Every cell is filled, so it looks complete. But the evidence is a feeling no screen can observe, and even if it could, dragging a label onto a plot demonstrates label placement, not interpretation. The screen is polished and the row is wrong. ECD exists to catch exactly this.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEvidence-Centered Design treats assessment, and by extension any prototype that claims to teach or measure, as an argument with three linked layers:\n\n| Layer | Question it answers | Failure mode |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Claim (student model) | What knowledge or skill are we asserting? | Vague or unmeasurable target |\n| Evidence (evidence model) | What observable behavior would reveal that knowledge? | Internal state instead of an action |\n| Task (task model) | What situation forces the learner to produce the observable? | Task elicits a different behavior than the one named |\n\nThe argument is only valid when the layers connect in both directions: the task must elicit the observable, and the observable must license the claim. A clean screen with a broken link is worse than an ugly screen with an intact one, because the clean screen invites false confidence.\n\nECD reasons backward, from what you want to claim to the screen that will prove it. Put the design steps in that backward order.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"State the claim: what knowledge or skill are we asserting (interpret a residual plot)?\", \"Specify the evidence: what scorable behavior would reveal that knowledge (select the correct diagnosis)?\", \"Design the task: what screen forces the learner to produce that observable (diagnosis-selection screen)?\", \"Audit both backward links: does the task elicit the observable, and does the observable license the claim?\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A row reads: claim 'learner can interpret a residual plot'; evidence 'learner completes the drag-the-label task'; task 'drag-the-label screen'. Which link is broken?\" explanation=\"Task completion is reachable by guessing or by interface familiarity, so completion does not license the interpretation claim. The observable should be a scorable judgment that requires the claimed skill, such as choosing the correct diagnosis.\" options='[{\"text\":\"None; every cell is filled, so the row is valid.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The observable-to-claim link: completing the task does not require interpreting the plot.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The claim is too specific to be testable.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe course anchors sharpen each layer. **Mayer's principles** inform the task layer: a task that buries the observable behavior under extraneous decoration raises load and contaminates the evidence. **Cognitive load theory** warns that a struggling learner may fail a task for load reasons unrelated to the claim, so a high-load task produces ambiguous evidence. **UDL learner variability** pushes you to ask whether the task elicits the observable for all intended learners or only for those who happen to match the interface's assumptions; a single task may under-represent the claim for some.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nWatch one matrix row move from misaligned to defensible.\n\n**Before (one matrix row, as first drafted):**\n\n| Claim | Evidence | Task screen |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Learner can interpret a residual plot | Learner feels confident about the plot | Drag-the-label screen |\n\n1. The evidence cell holds a feeling, which no screen can observe or score.\n2. The task elicits label placement, not interpretation, so it cannot supply evidence for this claim even in principle.\n3. Both backward links are broken: task does not yield the observable, observable does not license the claim.\n\n**After (same claim, realigned row):**\n\n| Claim | Evidence | Task screen |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Learner can interpret a residual plot | Learner selects the correct diagnosis (e.g., \"non-constant variance\") for three plotted patterns | Diagnosis-selection screen showing three residual plots |\n\nNow the chain holds: the diagnosis-selection task forces the learner to produce a scorable choice, the choice is an observable, and a correct diagnosis on varied patterns licenses the interpretation claim. A reviewer can audit the row without my narration.\n\nOne rejected suggestion: adding a \"How confident are you?\" prompt to the screen. I declined because confidence is not evidence of interpretation; it would add a data column the claim does not need and could tempt a later analyst to treat self-report as competence.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBuild every row backward, claim first, then evidence, then task, and accept the row only when both links survive an audit:\n\n1. **Observable check:** Is the evidence cell a behavior you could score from a screen recording, with no mind-reading? If it names a feeling or an internal state, rewrite it.\n2. **Elicitation check:** Does the task actually force the learner to produce that observable, or could a learner pass the task by some unrelated route? If the latter, the task is invalid for the claim.\n3. **Relevance check:** Does any data the task collects fail to support the claim? Drop or quarantine it so it cannot masquerade as evidence.\n\nA row that fails any check is not \"almost done\"; it is producing the wrong evidence, which is worse than producing none.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Audit an inherited ECD row\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"The row claims \\\"learner can write a testable hypothesis.\\\" The task is a four-option multiple-choice screen asking which hypothesis is best. The screen looks clean. What is your first move?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Approve it; all three cells are filled and the screen is polished.\",\"next\":\"approve\",\"feedback\":\"Filled cells are not a valid argument. The task elicits recognition, not production.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the elicitation check: could a learner pass without the claimed skill?\",\"next\":\"elicit\",\"feedback\":\"Right. This is the load-bearing audit for any ECD row.\"}]},\"approve\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer later asks how a recognition task licenses a writing claim. You have no answer. Back up and audit.\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Run the elicitation check.\",\"next\":\"elicit\",\"feedback\":\"This is where you should have started.\"}]},\"elicit\":{\"prompt\":\"You find that a learner can recognize a good hypothesis without being able to write one, so the observable is recognition, not production. What fix do you make?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Make the multiple-choice options prettier and add a confidence slider.\",\"next\":\"polish\",\"feedback\":\"Polish does not repair a broken elicitation link, and confidence is not evidence of the claim.\"},{\"label\":\"Change the task layer: give a scenario and ask the learner to type a hypothesis, scored for naming a manipulation, measure, and comparison.\",\"next\":\"fix\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Replacing recognition with construction restores the link to the writing claim.\"}]},\"polish\":{\"prompt\":\"The screen is now attractive but still measures recognition. The claim is still unsupported. What is the real fix?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Switch to a construction task scored against a rubric.\",\"next\":\"fix\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, the task layer, not the visual layer, is what was broken.\"}]},\"fix\":{\"prompt\":\"The construction task elicits a scorable typed hypothesis, the observable licenses the writing claim, and the matrix made the recognition/production mismatch visible before any learner saw the screen. The row is now defensible.\",\"feedback\":\"You repaired the row by fixing the task layer, not by polishing it.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading equates a more attractive task with a more valid one. Visual polish does not repair a broken elicitation link; a beautiful drag task still cannot measure interpretation. A second misreading treats \"the learner completed the task\" as evidence for the claim, when completion can be reached by guessing, by interface familiarity, or by a route that bypasses the target skill. ECD asks not whether the task was finished but whether finishing it required the claimed competence.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply ECD to a writing prototype. The claim is \"learner can write a testable hypothesis.\" A first draft task is a multiple-choice screen asking which of four hypotheses is best. Audit it: the observable in that task is recognition, not production, so it cannot license a *writing* claim; a learner could recognize a good hypothesis without being able to write one. The fix is a task-layer change, not a polish change: replace recognition with construction. The revised task gives a scenario and asks the learner to type a hypothesis, with the observable defined as \"the typed statement names a manipulation, a measure, and a comparison.\" Now the task elicits production, the observable is scorable against a rubric, and the chain to the claim is intact. The matrix made the recognition/production mismatch visible before any learner saw the screen.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead each row as an argument and try to break it. In the left column write the claim; in the right column write the single way a learner could satisfy the task *without* having the claimed skill. If you can find such a route, the row is invalid and the task layer needs work. A strong reader of an ECD matrix does not check whether cells are filled; they check whether the easiest path through the task happens to require the competence the claim asserts. When no shortcut exists, the evidence is trustworthy.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe traceability matrix is strong when every row survives the elicitation audit and when the README states, for at least one row, the alternative route a learner could have taken and how the task design forecloses it. Polish (typography, color, layout) belongs only after the three links hold. If a link is missing, annotate the cell and request a targeted comparison of a valid versus invalid task for that claim, rather than a general explanation of ECD.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry ECD into a setting where you inherit someone else's prototype and cannot rebuild the tasks. The matrix still earns its place: by reverse-engineering each screen into the claim-evidence-task layers, you can label which existing screens produce trustworthy evidence and which are decorative. That triage lets you spend a limited revision budget on the rows whose links are broken, rather than uniformly polishing screens that already work. The discipline transfers because it is about the logic of the evidence argument, not about who built the screens.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-02-evidence-centered-design-for-prototypes-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the prototype README traceability matrix connecting theory to screen changes for AIL 606 07.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links Evidence-Centered Design for Prototypes, evidence source, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates design learning from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","stem":"In evidence-centered design, a prototype README traceability matrix should connect three layers. Which ordering matches the ECD logic?","options":["Screen layout, then color palette, then font size.","Claim about what the learner knows or can do, then the observable evidence that would show it, then the task or screen that elicits that evidence.","Task screen, then a generic learning goal, then a deadline.","Evidence, then polish, then submission."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"ECD runs claim then evidence then task. The matrix is defensible only when each screen change traces up to an observable behavior and a knowledge claim.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_task_before_claim","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-01","ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","stem":"A matrix row reads: claim = \"learner can interpret a residual plot\"; evidence = \"clicks the correct diagnosis button\"; task = \"drag-the-label screen.\" What is the alignment defect?","options":["Nothing is wrong; all three cells are filled.","The task (drag-the-label) does not elicit the stated evidence (clicking a diagnosis button), so the task and evidence do not match.","The claim is too short.","The evidence should be a survey instead."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Evidence-centered design fails when the task cannot produce the observable named in the evidence cell. The drag task yields a different observable than the diagnosis click.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_filled_not_aligned","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You use an AI tool while building the traceability matrix. Which use keeps your design judgment in charge?","options":["Let the AI fill every claim, evidence, and task cell and ship the matrix.","Ask the AI to flag rows where the task could not actually produce the listed evidence, then decide each flagged row yourself against the prototype screen.","Skip the AI because traceability must be done by hand.","Use the AI only to reformat the table so it looks cleaner."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Bounded use targets one checkable property (task-elicits-evidence). You still judge each flag against the real screen, which is the ECD reasoning that cannot be delegated.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_task_before_claim","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-04"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","stem":"Your matrix lists five screen changes but only two trace up to a knowledge claim. What does that pattern tell a reviewer, and what should you do?","options":["It means the prototype is mostly done; submit it.","Three changes are unjustified by the evidence model, so either add the claim and observable each one serves or remove the change.","It means you need more screens.","It means the matrix should be deleted."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Screen changes with no upward trace are decoration, not evidence-bearing design. ECD asks you to justify or cut each one.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_filled_not_aligned","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ail606_supplement_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["ail606-supplement-07-02-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_task_before_claim","pattern":"task_designed_before_claim","description":"Building the prototype screen or task first and then back-filling a claim, so the task drives the evidence model instead of the reverse","trigger":"learner starts the matrix from the task column and invents a claim to justify a screen they already built","feedback":"Start from the claim about what the learner should know or do, derive the observable evidence, then design the task. The task is last in the ECD chain.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_filled_not_aligned","pattern":"filled_cells_treated_as_alignment","description":"Treating a fully populated traceability matrix as aligned without checking that each task actually elicits the evidence its row names","trigger":"learner points to completed cells but never checks whether the task could produce the listed observable","feedback":"A complete matrix is not an aligned matrix. Check each row: can this task produce exactly the evidence named, for this claim?","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_2_evidence_centered_design_for_prototypes_evidence_as_opinion","pattern":"evidence_cell_holds_opinion","description":"Writing a subjective impression in the evidence column instead of an observable learner behavior","trigger":"evidence cell says learners will feel confident or find it useful rather than naming an action that can be recorded","feedback":"The evidence cell must name something observable in the prototype, such as a selection, a sequence, or a constructed response, not a feeling.","rail_action":"explain"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"254da87fc77b7705436dcb4cd245931e966ea81b611617c34c84bf893450f15b","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}